WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.680
Improv can be found everywhere in&nbsp;
cinema. And not just in comedies!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.720
It can be dramatic or scary, or even in&nbsp;
a David Lynch flick. Some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:13.731
famous scenes in Hollywood history&nbsp;
have been completely off the cuff.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:19.140
Judd Apatow first made a name for himself in the&nbsp;
entertainment industry through stand-up comedy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:23.940
and later through television. After working on&nbsp;
the shows Freaks and Geeks, The Larry Sanders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:28.920
Show, and Undeclared, Apatow directed The&nbsp;
40-Year-Old Virgin as his feature film debut.

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:33.900
Apatow's filmmaking style has been largely&nbsp;
defined by improv comedy. As can be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.980
in the Line-O-Rama bonus features frequently&nbsp;
included on the DVD releases of his movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:44.760
improv is present on set in most scenes. Apatow's&nbsp;
scripts provide comedic situations and setups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:48.480
but he frequently allows his actors to play&nbsp;
around and generate different punchlines.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.960
Improv can be found throughout the&nbsp;
entirety of The 40-Year-Old Virgin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:57.840
but one scene in particular stands out from&nbsp;
the rest, the famous waxing scene. As shown in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.340
a behind-the-scenes featurette, Steve Carell&nbsp;
was actually waxed on camera to capture his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:07.140
authentic reactions. Five cameras were used&nbsp;
to record the whole scene at once as Carell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.000
and the other actors in the scene improvised&nbsp;
reactions in Apatow's typical Line-O-Rama style.

00:01:12.034 --> 00:01:16.987
"Wowee! That–' 'I'm sorry' 'I'm sorry, I&nbsp;
really don't swear this much.' 'Alright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.987 --> 00:01:19.617
you know what, I got a weak stomach.&nbsp;
That's all I can really take.'"

00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:24.480
As Apatow explained on The Rich Eisen Show,&nbsp;
Carell, who co-wrote the script with Apatow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.040
insisted on getting waxed for real. It&nbsp;
was also revealed that the woman who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.084
waxed Carell had never done it before&nbsp;
and lied to them during her audition.

00:01:33.084 --> 00:01:36.000
"The number one rule when you&nbsp;
wax is that you don't put the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.230
wax over the nipple. Because you&nbsp;
can just rip someone's nipple off."

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.980
Director John Cassavetes' [ka-suh-VEH-tees]&nbsp;
Faces was a game-changer for independent film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.980 --> 00:01:49.080
when it was released in 1968. Cassavetes&nbsp;
was already a successful actor known for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:54.600
his roles in films like The Dirty Dozen, The&nbsp;
Killers, and Rosemary's Baby. According to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:59.220
biography Accidental Genius by Marshall Fine,&nbsp;
Cassavetes used the money he earned as an actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:04.500
to self-finance his own films. He was able to work&nbsp;
completely outside of the studio system and create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.820
films that placed all emphasis on character&nbsp;
and acting while striving for authenticity.

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:13.560
Though it was his fourth time directing, Faces&nbsp;
was Cassavetes' first film to breakthrough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:18.480
to audiences and critics in a major way. The&nbsp;
film earned three Oscar nominations for actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.700
Seymour Cassel, Lynn Carlin and for original&nbsp;
screenplay. As good as his starting material was,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:28.980
it became stronger as it was explored through&nbsp;
rehearsals and on the set. Cassavetes gave his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:32.820
small troupe of actors a concrete script&nbsp;
but afforded them the freedom to explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.820 --> 00:02:36.600
the material from different angles.&nbsp;
Cassavetes himself contributed to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:41.220
improvisational spirit while behind the camera&nbsp;
by shouting out direction to the cast mid-scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:46.320
which he then had to figure out how to cover up&nbsp;
in post-production. At over two hours long, Faces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:51.634
is composed of just a handful of long scenes,&nbsp;
each which probe deep into the human psyche.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:57.300
Angel — not to be confused with the popular TV&nbsp;
show of the same name — is a sleazy exploitation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.580
film from 1983. The sensationalized plot&nbsp;
follows a 15-year-old high schooler who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:07.140
moonlights as a sex worker. She finds herself&nbsp;
in danger when a deranged serial killer begins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.640
targeting her fellow coworkers. The unnamed&nbsp;
serial killer character was an early role for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:16.140
actor John Diehl, who would later appear in&nbsp;
bigger projects like The Shield and Stargate.

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:21.180
As explained by Joe Bob Briggs when he hosted&nbsp;
Angel for an episode of his The Last Drive In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:26.340
series, Diehl was responsible for improvising&nbsp;
the film's single most memorable moment. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:31.020
had zero dialogue in the film, so Diehl faced&nbsp;
a major challenge when it came to making his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:35.940
character memorable and effectively disturbing&nbsp;
in only a handful of scenes. During auditions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.440
director Robert Vincent O'Neil simply asked the&nbsp;
prospective actors to be creepy without speaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:45.240
Diehl rose to this task by bringing an egg with&nbsp;
him, poking a hole in the top with a knife,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:49.740
and sucking the yoke out before crushing&nbsp;
the shell against his mouth. O'Neil was so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:55.020
impressed by Diehl's improvised audition that&nbsp;
an egg-sucking scene was added into the movie.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:59.417
"I never saw it to fail. Give a man&nbsp;
something to suck on and he's happy."

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:05.160
In director Mike Leigh's 2004 drama Vera Drake,&nbsp;
the titular character is a working-class family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:09.480
woman who provides abortions unbeknownst&nbsp;
to her family at a time when the practice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:14.460
was illegal in the U.K. Imelda Staunton earned&nbsp;
an Oscar nomination for her lead performance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:19.620
and Leigh earned two Oscar nominations in the&nbsp;
directing and writing categories. While the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:23.460
first two nominations were well-deserved,&nbsp;
the third likely came as a surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:26.280
considering the film didn't&nbsp;
have a screenplay at first.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:31.140
Improvisation is at the heart of Leigh's&nbsp;
directorial approach. Staunton explained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:33.660
that all she knew at the start of&nbsp;
the project was that it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.800
set in the 1950s and that it would&nbsp;
explore the subject of abortion.

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:41.880
"'Ms. Wells, if you were to have the child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.401
would you keep it or have it&nbsp;
adopted?' 'I can't have it.'"

00:04:45.401 --> 00:04:50.340
Everything else started from a blank slate, as&nbsp;
she and Leigh began rehearsing and improvising&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:54.900
the piece from scratch for a full six&nbsp;
months. Staunton had never improvised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:59.100
before but dove into the deep end and&nbsp;
ultimately found the process exhilarating.

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.600
The film's standout scene is the birthday&nbsp;
party in which the police unexpectedly arrive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:08.100
and Vera's secret activities come to light. As&nbsp;
revealed in the book Mike Leigh on Mike Leigh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.100 --> 00:05:12.360
none of the actors playing the family had&nbsp;
any idea the police were about to show up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.900
Only Staunton knew that her character&nbsp;
was performing abortions at that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:20.340
and the shocked reactions of everyone&nbsp;
else were 100% authentic. In the book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:25.534
Leigh described the scene as the result of an&nbsp;
improvisation that ran for 10 hours straight.

00:05:25.680 --> 00:05:30.480
"'I don't understand it.' 'I&nbsp;
don't expect you to, Sid.'"

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:34.560
Quentin Tarantino is highly&nbsp;
valued for his skills as a writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.020
so his sets are rarely home to improvisation.&nbsp;
But there have been a few exceptions. One of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.020 --> 00:05:44.100
the biggest improvised moments arose out of an&nbsp;
accident during the filming of Django Unchained.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:48.960
During one of the film's most intense scenes,&nbsp;
Calvin Candie, played by Leonardo DiCaprio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:52.440
rants about phrenology
and forcibly auctions off Broomhilda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:57.540
played by Kerry Washington. DiCaprio told ABC&nbsp;
News that a glass shattered and cut his hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.540 --> 00:06:01.200
when he slammed the tabletop. The delayed&nbsp;
moment when he realized he was bleeding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.640
was kept in the final edit, and DiCaprio was&nbsp;
able to stay in character and finish out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.080
scene as his hand gushed blood. The bloody hand&nbsp;
was incorporated into the rest of the sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:13.740
with Candie even smearing his blood all&nbsp;
over Broomhilda's face at one point.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:18.060
It's entirely possible that his wound was&nbsp;
cleaned and fake blood was applied to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:23.340
hand instead of DiCaprio rubbing his actual&nbsp;
blood all over Washington's face. But the idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:26.400
at least, stemmed from the unexpected injury.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.380
From the very first scene of Apocalypse Now, the&nbsp;
production was already unfolding in an unorthodox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:35.700
manner. The opening scene, which finds Captain&nbsp;
Willard drunk and freaking out in a hotel room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:40.920
wasn't originally in the script. It was entirely&nbsp;
improvised by Martin Sheen while he was liquored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:45.780
up for real. During an appearance at the Dubai&nbsp;
International Film Festival many years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.320
Sheen explained that the scene was shot&nbsp;
on his birthday and that he had spent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:53.880
the entire day drinking before shooting&nbsp;
even started. He was open about the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.880 --> 00:06:58.080
that he was in the throes of alcoholism at&nbsp;
the time and didn't get sober until 1989.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:00.840
Sheen described his mental state&nbsp;
during the filming of that scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:03.240
as being extremely precarious. He recalled:

00:07:03.240 --> 00:07:08.820
"I was so fragmented, I didn't have a clue&nbsp;
who I was. I said let's film this. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:13.200
so hopelessly drunk I couldn't stand up, but I&nbsp;
had a sense that I could deal with this demon."

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:16.800
He described his performance as being&nbsp;
less about that character of Willard and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.640
more about his own personal struggle with&nbsp;
addiction. Director Francis Ford Coppola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.360
tried to stop him in the improvised scene&nbsp;
when he accidentally broke the mirror and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:29.160
injured his hand. But Sheen insisted on&nbsp;
finishing the scene on his own terms. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:33.780
blood that pours from his hand and&nbsp;
stains the bed sheet was 100% real.

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:38.940
The 1996 movie Secrets and Lies tells the story&nbsp;
of a well-off Black woman who discovers after her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:43.800
adoptive parents die that her birth mother is&nbsp;
a working-class white woman. It became one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:47.520
the most lauded films of the year. It won&nbsp;
three awards at the Cannes Film Festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:53.040
including the Palme d'Or, was nominated for&nbsp;
five Academy Awards, and took home three BAFTAs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.040 --> 00:07:57.540
including the best British film award.&nbsp;
And it won for original screenplay. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.540 --> 00:08:02.280
as is usually the case with director Mike Leigh's&nbsp;
films, there was no screenplay when filming began.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:06.180
Leigh spoke at length about the creation&nbsp;
of Secrets and Lies during an appearance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:10.440
at Film at Lincoln Center. He explained that&nbsp;
he assembled his cast before any characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:15.180
or even any real semblance of the plot had&nbsp;
been formed, beyond knowing that he wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:19.534
explore the subject of adoption. Together&nbsp;
they improvised what Leigh described as:

00:08:19.534 --> 00:08:27.214
"A whole world, from which we are then&nbsp;
able to distill and structure the film."

00:08:27.214 --> 00:08:31.260
The moment when the mother-daughter strangers&nbsp;
meet for the first time is heartbreaking in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:35.040
its realism and simplicity. As the&nbsp;
two sit side-by-side in a diner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.900
the camera captures their entire interaction in&nbsp;
a single long take that lasts for about eight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:44.940
minutes before cutting. Leigh's improvisational&nbsp;
approach enables a lengthy, dialogue-heavy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.940 --> 00:08:49.080
scene such as this to unfold naturally in&nbsp;
a way that wouldn't be possible otherwise.

00:08:49.371 --> 00:08:52.746
"'I'm a bit of a disappointment to you, ain't I?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.746 --> 00:08:58.080
'No.' 'You don't have to say&nbsp;
that, darling.' 'I know.'"

00:08:58.980 --> 00:09:03.660
Sidney Lumet's Dog Day Afternoon tells the true&nbsp;
story of a man who attempts a bank robbery so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:08.100
that he can afford gender-affirming surgery&nbsp;
for his partner. Al Pacino and John Cazale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:12.060
star as the bank robber and his accomplice&nbsp;
respectively, and the two had just starred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.060 --> 00:09:16.920
together the previous year in The Godfather&nbsp;
Part II. Lumet was a filmmaker who previously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:22.500
held a strict no improvisation policy, but&nbsp;
Dog Day Afternoon changed his entire mindset.

00:09:22.500 --> 00:09:27.180
Lumet explained in his book Making Movies,&nbsp;
that Frank Pierson's adapted screenplay was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.180 --> 00:09:31.500
used for the structure, but the dialogue was&nbsp;
largely thrown out in favor of allowing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:36.360
actors to improvise their way through scenes&nbsp;
with utmost naturalism being the goal. About&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.360 --> 00:09:42.720
60% of the final film was fully improvised. When&nbsp;
speaking to Larry King on CNN, Pacino confirmed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:47.647
that even the film's biggest and most iconic&nbsp;
moments came from improvisation. The most well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.647 --> 00:09:53.040
known scene where he shouts "Attica!" and riles up&nbsp;
the crowd of onlookers was completely improvised.

00:09:53.244 --> 00:09:57.204
"Attica! Attica! Attica! Attica!"

00:09:57.466 --> 00:10:01.860
The idea of invoking the real-life&nbsp;
Attica prison uprising as a thematic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:06.405
connection to the bank robbery came from&nbsp;
Lumet's assistant director, Burtt Harris.

00:10:06.405 --> 00:10:12.360
One of John Cassavetes's greatest achievements&nbsp;
as a filmmaker was his 1974 drama A Woman Under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:17.100
the Influence. Cassavetes and Gena Rowlands&nbsp;
— his real-life wife and favorite leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:21.240
lady — both earned Oscar nominations&nbsp;
for their work. This character-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.240 --> 00:10:25.620
story of domestication, marital strife,&nbsp;
and mental health struggles unfolds with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:30.660
Cassavetes's typical long and winding scenes&nbsp;
performed exhaustively with hyper-realism.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:35.040
As Kent Jones wrote in an essay for the&nbsp;
Criterion Collection, the film defies:

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:39.480
"A century's worth of film theory,&nbsp;
screenwriting tips, and film school orthodoxy."

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:44.700
In the book Cassavetes on Cassavetes, the&nbsp;
director revealed that his unorthodox process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.700 --> 00:10:48.060
meant that A Woman Under the Influence&nbsp;
took more than three months of shooting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:53.220
with six day work weeks to complete. Some&nbsp;
believed his films to be 100% improvised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.940
but he dispelled that notion, saying&nbsp;
instead that he always brought the cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.940 --> 00:11:01.740
a complete screenplay. Only then would it morph&nbsp;
significantly as scenes changed and evolved.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:05.940
He described the process as sticking to certain&nbsp;
lines while allowing the actors to interpret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.940 --> 00:11:10.500
them however they saw fit and then build upon&nbsp;
the material. He wanted the actors to respond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:16.020
to whatever happened in the moment as if they were&nbsp;
reacting naturally in real life. Cassavetes added:

00:11:16.020 --> 00:11:21.000
"It really is a product of a group of people&nbsp;
coming in and interpreting their roles. Really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.560
truthfully interpreting their roles.&nbsp;
Everything that Gena did she did herself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:29.355
I don't try and control the scene,&nbsp;
which is often confused, anarchic."

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.860
Kramer vs. Kramer tells the messy and honest&nbsp;
story of a couple's divorce and their battle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.860 --> 00:11:38.820
over the custody of their child. Dustin&nbsp;
Hoffman and Meryl Streep are in peak form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.820 --> 00:11:42.540
as the two halves of this destroyed&nbsp;
marriage. Both took home Oscars for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.540 --> 00:11:47.830
their performances as part of the film's five&nbsp;
wins and nine total Academy Award nominations.

00:11:47.830 --> 00:11:50.280
"'I'm leaving you.' 'Honey,&nbsp;
please. I can't hear.'"

00:11:50.280 --> 00:11:53.820
Robert Benton's Oscar-winning&nbsp;
screenplay provided a wealth of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:57.420
juicy material for the actors to sink&nbsp;
their teeth into. But as a director,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.420 --> 00:12:01.500
Benton brought a sense of spontaneity to&nbsp;
the table that Hoffman fully embraced.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:04.320
There are various instances&nbsp;
of improvisation that can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.320 --> 00:12:08.460
spotted throughout Kramer vs. Kramer. The&nbsp;
most noteworthy ones arise in explosive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:13.200
scenes between Hoffman and Streep. Some of&nbsp;
Hoffman's improvisation crossed the line,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:17.880
like when he slapped Streep right as a take&nbsp;
started and smashed a glass against the wall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.880 --> 00:12:22.980
beside her head near the end of a scene without&nbsp;
warning. He also improvised some cruel lines that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:27.240
doubled as insulting personal comments about&nbsp;
Streep's boyfriend — fellow actor John Cazale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.240 --> 00:12:32.640
who had recently died of lung cancer — all to&nbsp;
force genuine reactions out of her. Hoffman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.640 --> 00:12:36.840
used similar tactics on eight-year-old actor&nbsp;
Justin Henry as well, who became the youngest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.840 --> 00:12:42.600
Oscar nominee ever. Henry was never given a&nbsp;
script, and his scenes were entirely improvised.

00:12:43.320 --> 00:12:47.820
The action-packed Police Story is a&nbsp;
Hong Kong kung-fu crime film from 1985,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.820 --> 00:12:52.260
filled with goofy humor and jaw-dropping&nbsp;
stunts in equal measure. Jackie Chan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.260 --> 00:12:56.100
not only stars in the lead role but&nbsp;
also co-wrote and directed the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:59.940
delivering one of the greatest films in&nbsp;
his all-around impressive body of work.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:05.160
The film's lengthy opening action sequence begins&nbsp;
with a shootout, moves to an explosive car chase,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:10.500
then progresses to Chan chasing a double-decker&nbsp;
bus on foot. All of these dangerous stunts were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:14.760
precisely coordinated, but the stunt that&nbsp;
concluded the sequence didn't go according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:19.560
to plan. As Chan himself explained, when the&nbsp;
bus stops, three stuntmen were supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.560 --> 00:13:23.700
crash through the windows and land on the&nbsp;
car parked in front of it. But the bus's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.700 --> 00:13:27.600
brakes didn't act as anticipated and sent the&nbsp;
stunt performers through the windows too early.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:30.600
Landing atop a car might sound extremely painful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:35.160
but the slight springiness to the metal and the&nbsp;
added height of the vehicle were preferable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.160 --> 00:13:39.960
slamming directly onto the concrete far&nbsp;
below, which is what ended up happening.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.960 --> 00:13:44.400
Rather than squander the stunt team's pain&nbsp;
by calling cut, Chan was able to stay in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:49.334
character and improvise the rest of the shot,&nbsp;
incorporating the accident into the final film.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:55.560
After the big-budget disaster of his 1984 Dune&nbsp;
adaptation, David Lynch reteamed with star Kyle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:59.940
MacLachlan for a smaller film with a twisted&nbsp;
original script that only Lynch could deliver.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.940 --> 00:14:06.060
The 1986 film Blue Velvet is a nightmarish descent&nbsp;
into the disturbing crimes and perversions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:10.800
fester below the surface of suburban America,&nbsp;
as told with Lynch's signature surrealistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:15.540
style. His work rarely adheres to the rules&nbsp;
of the natural world and is often derived&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:19.980
from dreams, the subconscious, and through&nbsp;
his practice of transcendental meditation.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:25.260
In Blue Velvet, music is a key component and&nbsp;
even inspired its title, with the Bobby Vinton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.260 --> 00:14:29.520
song of the same name sung by Isabella&nbsp;
Rossellini in the film. Another important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:34.140
musical number occurs when Dean Stockwell&nbsp;
performs Roy Orbison's "In Dreams" into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:38.640
construction light. In the book Lynch on&nbsp;
Lynch, it was revealed that a version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:42.600
this scene was planned but that the end&nbsp;
result came about through improvisation.

00:14:42.600 --> 00:14:46.680
The initial plan was for Dennis Hopper to&nbsp;
perform the song as the film's villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:51.060
Frank Booth. Hopper and Stockwell began&nbsp;
singing it together on set despite only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:55.980
Hopper being on camera. Hopper was so taken&nbsp;
by Stockwell's performance that he stopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:01.080
singing and simply watched him finish the song.&nbsp;
Lynch loved the result and reworked the scene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:06.060
The choice to sing into a construction light&nbsp;
was another spontaneous decision, as Stockwell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:10.260
mistakenly grabbed a light used by the crew&nbsp;
instead of the prop he was supposed to use.

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:16.320
After making Husbands and before making A Woman&nbsp;
Under the Influence, director John Cassavetes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.320 --> 00:15:22.020
released Minnie and Moskowitz. The film utilized&nbsp;
his unique hyper-naturalistic style but injected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.020 --> 00:15:26.820
it into the structure of an old-school screwball&nbsp;
romantic comedy that eschewed all the rules.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.820 --> 00:15:32.040
The titular characters of Minnie, played by Gena&nbsp;
Rowlands, and Moskowitz, played by Seymour Cassel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:36.660
form a troubled romance as colorful supporting&nbsp;
characters breeze in and out of their lives.

00:15:36.660 --> 00:15:41.640
As with his other films, Cassavetes gave&nbsp;
his actors as much latitude as possible to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:44.640
flesh out the characters themselves&nbsp;
as they were shooting their scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.640 --> 00:15:49.080
But by placing his improvisational style&nbsp;
into a more conventional structure – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.080 --> 00:15:52.800
framework of a traditional romantic&nbsp;
comedy — Cassavetes was able to break&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:52.800 --> 00:15:57.060
through to wider audiences with Minnie and&nbsp;
Moskowitz as opposed to his other films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.060 --> 00:16:01.680
which were frequently labeled by his critics&nbsp;
as self-indulgent and meandering to a fault.

00:16:02.580 --> 00:16:06.660
Given how meticulously choreographed&nbsp;
and executed the John Wick movies are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:11.460
it might come as a surprise to learn that&nbsp;
improvisation still made its way into John Wick:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:17.040
Chapter 3 — Parabellum. Mark Dacascos, who plays&nbsp;
the film's lead villain Zero, revealed to Vulture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:22.020
that his character was written to be familiar with&nbsp;
Mr. Wick's reputation. But Dacascos wanted to push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.020 --> 00:16:27.660
that part of his character further. In their first&nbsp;
face-to-face scene, Dacascos began to improvise by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.660 --> 00:16:32.640
moving uncomfortably close to Keanu Reeves, to the&nbsp;
point that their legs were touching. He explained:

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:36.120
"I could feel Keanu's energy, it&nbsp;
felt like his energy was saying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:41.880
'What the f—,' because my leg is touching his.&nbsp;
Big kudos and credit to Keanu and Chad [Stahelski,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.880 --> 00:16:45.900
director] because either of them could&nbsp;
have said, 'Cut' [...] But Chad let it go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.900 --> 00:16:49.740
and Keanu stayed in character."
 
As the scene went on, Reeves picked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:54.300
up on what Dacascos was doing and joined in&nbsp;
until the scene was finished. Dacascos recalled:

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:57.060
"There's a beat, and I hear Chad from the back go,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:57.060 --> 00:16:59.940
'I love it. We're doing it.' And&nbsp;
that's how that take evolved."

00:17:00.660 --> 00:17:05.040
Mikey and Nicky is an exploration of a toxic&nbsp;
friendship from writer-director Elaine May.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:05.040 --> 00:17:09.720
The film follows two low-level gangsters&nbsp;
with a long and complicated history that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:14.220
bubbles back to the surface when Nicky becomes&nbsp;
an assassination target. May had a background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.220 --> 00:17:19.320
in improvisation as part of the improv comedy&nbsp;
duo Nichols and May, so her casting improvisers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:19.320 --> 00:17:23.400
Peter Falk and John Cassavetes in the&nbsp;
two titular roles was no coincidence.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:27.780
May's screenplay was written in an unusual&nbsp;
style that was conducive to her and her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.780 --> 00:17:31.620
cast's exploratory approach. The opening&nbsp;
sequence is a perfect example of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:31.620 --> 00:17:35.520
as the first four-and-a-half pages are&nbsp;
written more like a book than a script.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:40.020
Background information, character details, and&nbsp;
future-tense occurrences are described without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:40.020 --> 00:17:44.520
explicitly telling the actors what to do or&nbsp;
how. The first lines of the film are Nicky&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:49.140
giving Mikey instructions over the phone, but the&nbsp;
dialogue itself was left completely unscripted.

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:54.060
May's process for making the film typically&nbsp;
involved letting Falk and Cassavetes improvise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:57.720
with three cameras rolling. It wasn't&nbsp;
uncommon for multiple hours to pass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:02.520
between May calling "action" and "cut." Though&nbsp;
the film is only an hour and 46 minutes long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:08.040
May shot a staggering 1.4 million feet of&nbsp;
film stock, which is equivalent to around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.040 --> 00:18:10.788
14,000 hours' worth of footage.

00:18:11.034 --> 00:18:15.240
Kill Zone is a gritty police thriller&nbsp;
from Hong Kong. Simon Yam and Donnie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.660
Yen star as a police inspector nearing&nbsp;
retirement and his incoming replacement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:24.240
both of whom become obsessed with taking down a&nbsp;
crime kingpin played by martial arts legend Sammo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.240 --> 00:18:29.220
Hung. The first huge action scene finds Donnie&nbsp;
Yen squaring off in an alleyway against Wu Jing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:35.100
who would later star in and direct China's highest&nbsp;
grossing film of all time, Wolf Warrior 2. Given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:35.100 --> 00:18:39.720
the intense coordination and danger involved,&nbsp;
it's exceptionally rare to find improvisation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.720 --> 00:18:45.180
in a fight sequence, but Kill Zone pulled it off.&nbsp;
As shown in the behind-the-scenes documentary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.180 --> 00:18:49.140
the alleyway baton-vs-knife fight&nbsp;
featured extensive improvisation.

00:18:49.140 --> 00:18:53.700
But the alleyway sequence wasn't in the script,&nbsp;
and the first cut of the film had been completed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:58.740
without any fight scenes in it. Yen realized the&nbsp;
movie was in need of action, so he took it upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.740 --> 00:19:04.140
himself to get additional funding and spend five&nbsp;
days shooting the alleyway fight on the fly. Yen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.140 --> 00:19:08.160
explained that he would have certain moves planned&nbsp;
out in his head but that he would implement them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.160 --> 00:19:12.660
in different orders and combinations while&nbsp;
sparring with Jing on camera. He explained:

00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:16.560
"It's like musicians, you jam, you&nbsp;
know? You see all these movements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:18.420
they're really not choreographed."

00:19:19.200 --> 00:19:23.640
Director Mike Leigh and star David Thewlis both&nbsp;
took home the top honors in their categories at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:28.620
the Cannes Film Festival for their work on the&nbsp;
1993 drama Naked. Thewlis plays a nihilistic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.620 --> 00:19:33.840
drifter, conspiracy theorist, and all-around&nbsp;
terrible person in a loose narrative that follows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:38.340
his exploits through London in the middle of the&nbsp;
night. As is always the case with Leigh's films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.340 --> 00:19:42.900
the entire project was born from extensive&nbsp;
improvisation, research, and rehearsals.

00:19:42.900 --> 00:19:46.980
He and Thewlis had collaborated twice&nbsp;
before on other projects but Thewlis's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.980 --> 00:19:51.120
screentime was brief on both occasions.&nbsp;
With virtually nothing other than wanting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.120 --> 00:19:54.240
to work together again on a project&nbsp;
in which Thewlis would be the lead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:58.620
the two generated the main character and&nbsp;
improvised a story through multiple months&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:02.760
of rehearsals. Leigh explained that he&nbsp;
treated the character of Johnny as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:07.800
receptacle for information and piled mountains&nbsp;
of research about various subjects onto Thewlis.

00:20:07.800 --> 00:20:12.300
This all came in handy and allowed Thewlis to&nbsp;
go wild in the memorable sequence in which he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.300 --> 00:20:16.440
befriends a security guard and rants about&nbsp;
numerous subjects and conspiracy theories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:21.240
such as the impending Y2K hysteria and&nbsp;
the theory that barcodes were satanic.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:27.780
With 11 Oscar nominations and six wins including&nbsp;
best picture, The Godfather Part II managed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:27.780 --> 00:20:31.740
miraculous feat of outdoing the original&nbsp;
best picture-winning The Godfather at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:36.360
Academy Awards. Released just two short&nbsp;
years later, this follow-up acts as both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:41.280
a sequel and a prequel to the first film as it&nbsp;
tracks Michael Corleone's growing ruthlessness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:41.280 --> 00:20:45.120
while showcasing Vito Corleone's rise&nbsp;
to power through multiple flashbacks.

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:50.220
One of the film's strongest and most emotional&nbsp;
scenes is the final confrontation between Michael&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:50.220 --> 00:20:54.840
and his older brother Fredo, played by John&nbsp;
Cazale. The scene was already powerful in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:54.840 --> 00:20:58.800
script, but it was made infinitely stronger&nbsp;
through an element that wasn't on the page.

00:20:58.800 --> 00:21:04.260
Cazale's choice to play the entire scene from&nbsp;
a bouncy, low-backed chair was a perfect fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.260 --> 00:21:08.880
for the scene. The awkward position made his&nbsp;
outrage all the more pathetic and ineffectual.

00:21:08.880 --> 00:21:11.334
"I'm smart and I want respect!"

00:21:11.506 --> 00:21:14.940
On a commentary track recorded for the&nbsp;
physical media release of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:14.940 --> 00:21:18.000
director Francis Ford Coppola&nbsp;
explained that the chair just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:21.720
happened to be on the set and wound up&nbsp;
becoming a vital part of it, saying:

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:25.140
"There was something about that chair that&nbsp;
worked so great for the scene ... the chair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.140 --> 00:21:30.000
just kind of made him be so limp and springy&nbsp;
... sometimes you get lucky with a chair."

00:21:30.547 --> 00:21:36.420
They Live is a 1988 sci-fi action classic&nbsp;
from director John Carpenter. Roddy Piper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:40.440
plays a homeless man who comes into possession&nbsp;
of a pair of sunglasses that reveals the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:45.534
as it truly is, a capitalist dystopia&nbsp;
ruled by aliens disguised as humans.

00:21:45.534 --> 00:21:46.914
"What's your problem?"

00:21:46.914 --> 00:21:50.460
And both of the film's two most famous&nbsp;
scenes were the result of improvisation.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:54.840
If there is any scene from They Live that&nbsp;
the average person will be familiar with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:57.634
it's the one featuring the iconic line:

00:21:57.634 --> 00:22:06.900
"I have come here to chew bubblegum and&nbsp;
kick ass. And I'm all outta bubblegum."

00:22:06.900 --> 00:22:09.300
And Piper completely improvised the line.

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:14.220
The other well-known scene is the back alley&nbsp;
beatdown between Piper and co-star Keith David.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:19.020
In an interview with YouTuber Rossatron, Carpenter&nbsp;
and David explained that the fight scene was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:24.000
shoved into the movie after Piper had been cast.&nbsp;
Piper was better known as a wrestler with the WWF,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.140
where he went by the name "Rowdy" Roddy&nbsp;
Piper, and Carpenter figured that a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.140 --> 00:22:32.040
starring a professional wrestler ought to&nbsp;
make use of his physical abilities in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.040 --> 00:22:37.080
big fight scene that lasts for a whopping six&nbsp;
minutes. The 1988 shooting script simply read:

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:41.700
"And we begin the fight in the alley, as Nada&nbsp;
and Frank go at each other..." followed by three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:46.740
mostly blank pages on which only "The fight.&nbsp;
Continued," and "The Fight. Still Continued"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:46.740 --> 00:22:51.000
is written, with all of the intricacies&nbsp;
of the clash being worked out on location.

